[These comments are from playing a low pl 3.0 w/o using spoilers or
reading the code.  Please mark spoilers accordingly and use ^L...]

Some of the following may be considered spoilers.

At the pl I'm at, I can't kick anything except doors.  It seems like you
should be able to kick a sink and knock it off it's fitting.  When that 
happens, either (a) you create a pool or pools, from the outflowing water
or (b) it was a dry sink, and you can now fish around in the drain pipe
to get any rings that were dropped down the sink and maybe a ring or 
two left there by someone else (i.e., they would be left at that spot
on the floor).  

Empty bottles would be useful for getting potions of water out of sinks
or fountains.

In 3.1, how about having the character start in a big shop with some
starting money, and let him outfit himself.  This is an idea that was
used in srogue (?).

Perhaps I'm playing the HARD version, but I'm getting a little tired
of getting down to dlvl 5 and getting jumped by a couple of swarms of
osquips, killer bees and the occasional insane dwarf lord.  Someone
asked some time ago about situations in which Nethack unfairly kills
you - how about coming down to a level and having swarms of osquips
and killer bees come in the doors?  In general, I think 3.0 uses
"swarms" too much.  (Albeit I started that trend - it may even be my
own code I'm objecting too :-)

The balance between armor and hitting seems to be way off.  You need
some *seriously* negative armor to have much affect on monster's
chances to hit.  Monsters like killer bees and rothes don't have much
problem with -11, at least in my experience.  On the other hand, I
can't seem to hit much of anything - a level 5 samurai with a +2 sword
seems to hit killer bees about 1 in 20.   On the other hand, my 6 hp
dog doesn't seem to have much trouble mowing through hordes of monsters.

Your God should protect you against attacks during your prayer.  

New idea:  buried treasure.  This would be a chest with something inside
of it buried somewhere on the level.  It can be gotten by using a pickaxe
to create a pit, at which point the treasure would be in the pit (i.e., 
uncovered).  Dogs might notice that something was buried at a particular
spot and hang around there;  if there is tripe in the treasure they'll
try and dig it up (though that would take them a long time).

I think it is a little ridiculous that a floating eye that has been
paralyzed by its own gaze *and* slept can still paralyze you.  I don't
believe that gaze weapons should be *that* passive.

Possible bug: While I was blinded w/ esp, a rock piercer dropped down
on me, appearing in the square next to me.  I swung at it, and it
disappeared, only to immediately drop down on me again.  This happened
repeatedly until a tame pet came into the room and managed to get next
to the piercer.  This seems like a bug.  It also seems buggy that you
can't see a piercer on the ceiling w/ esp.

Another possible bug:  A tame rothe was "slept" by a homonculus but 
was able to continue attacking.  I don't know what the minimum sleep is
for a homonculus bite, but it didn't seem to have any affect on the rothe.

Yet another possible bug:  An Elf turned into a rat still has searching 
ability.

With the exception of magic traps you should be able to avoid traps
once you've found them.  There isn't any real justification for having
the player fall into a pit and damage himself when he knows there's a
pit there.  Dart and arrow traps should also have a very limited
number of charges (1-4?).

Standing on an altar of your deity should be like "Scare Monster".

Stopping to avoid your little dog shouldn't cost you a turn.  I
realize that you can use '/' to avoid this problem but that's a pain
in the butt.

